Binary search is a search algorithm that runs in O(log n)O(logn)
in the worst case, where
n is the size of the search space. For binary search to work, your search space usually needs to be sorted.
Binary search trees, which we looked at in the trees and graphs chapter, are based on binary search.


Normally, binary search is done on an array of sorted elements, but you can use binary search in more creative ways,
as we'll see in an upcoming article. For now, we'll just talk about sorted arrays of numbers

usecases :
If you have a sorted array arr and an element x, then in
O
(
log
⁡
n
)
O(logn) time and
O
(
1
)
O(1) space, binary search can:

Find the index of x if it is in arr
Find the first or the last index in which x can be inserted to maintain being sorted otherwis